---
<p class="lede">Until now every program was one file. This chapter splits a program into modules, decides what each module shows the others, and lays it out as a project: a <code>money</code> module, an <code>orders</code> module that uses it, and an app tying them together.</p>

<h2 id="directory">A directory is a module</h2>
<p>A module is a directory of <code>.byn</code> files: no <code>module</code> keyword, no header at the top of a file. Every file in it belongs to the same module and sees the others' declarations without an import. Split it as you like; the compiler sees one set of names.</p>
<p>So two files in one directory cannot both declare <code>grade</code>, even as separate programs: <code>bayan run</code> on a directory compiles all of it. That is why every example on this site runs from a directory of its own.</p>
<p>A module is named after its directory: <code>import money</code> brings in <code>money/</code>. The entry module holds the program's top-level statements; the others hold declarations.</p>

<h2 id="project">Make a project</h2>
<p><code>bayan new</code> creates a directory with a manifest, a source folder and a test folder.</p>
<pre><code>bayan new tidy
cd tidy</code></pre>
<pre><code>.editorconfig
.gitattributes
.gitignore
README.md
docs/
package.byn
src/app/main.byn
testdata/
tests/app.test.byn</code></pre>
<p><code>package.byn</code> is the manifest: data, not code, a <code>Package</code> literal with the name, version, edition, kind (<code>.app</code> or <code>.lib</code>) and effects it may use. <code>src/</code> holds the modules: an app's entry module is <code>src/app/</code>, and any other directory there is a module you import by its path. <code>tests/</code> holds test files (chapter 12).</p>
<p>Inside a project, <code>bayan run</code>, <code>bayan build</code> and <code>bayan test</code> need no path: they use the current directory's <code>package.byn</code>.</p>

<h2 id="import">Add a module and import it</h2>
<p>Create <code>src/money/money.byn</code>. The names it offers to other modules are marked <code>public</code>.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">/// An amount in cents.
public struct Money {
    private i32 cents

    public static Money dollars(i32 amount) =&gt; Money { cents: amount * 100 }

    public Money adding(Money other) =&gt; Money { cents: cents + other.cents }

    public i32 total() =&gt; cents
}</code></pre>
<p>The file name does not matter to the compiler, only the directory; naming the file after the module helps you find it.</p>
<p>Use it from <code>src/app/main.byn</code>. An <code>import</code> goes at the top of a file; the module's name then qualifies what you take from it.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">import money

let price = money.Money.dollars(3).adding(money.Money.dollars(4))
print($"{price.total()}")</code></pre>
<p><code>money.Money</code> is the type; <code>money.Money.dollars(3)</code> its static method. An import applies to the file that writes it, so a second file wanting <code>money</code> needs its own <code>import money</code>.</p>
<p>Rename a module with <code>as</code> when its name is long or two would clash.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">import money as m

let price: m.Money = m.Money.dollars(5)</code></pre>
<p>You import a whole module, never a single name: <code>import money.Money</code> is read as a module path and fails with BYN2004, <code>no module `money.Money`</code>. Write <code>import money</code>, then <code>money.Money</code>.</p>

<h2 id="visibility">Choose what a module shows</h2>
<p>Every declaration is one of three visibilities.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Written</th><th>Visible to</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>private</code></td><td>the type that declares it (a field or method)</td></tr>
<tr><td>nothing</td><td>the module: every file in the directory</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>public</code></td><td>other modules and packages</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Leave the keyword out and the name stays inside its module, the default for functions, types and fields: a module shows nothing until you decide it should. Forget <code>public</code> and the compiler says so. Here <code>money</code> declares <code>round</code> without it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">import money
let c = money.round(1)</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2005:</span> `round` is not visible here
  --&gt; main.byn:2:15

  2 │ let c = money.round(1)
    │               ^^^^^ not `public`
    │
  help: mark `round` `public` to use it from another module</pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler, from the test <code>tests/cases/diag/not-visible</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Module constants (<code>const rate: i32 = 8</code>) have no <code>public</code> form today; asking for one elsewhere gives the same error under a different label, <code>module constants are not visible outside their module</code>. Return the value from a <code>public</code> function instead.</p>
<p>Types and their fields follow the same rule. <code>orders</code> declares <code>struct Draft { string note }</code> with no <code>public</code>, so another module can neither name the type nor build it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">import orders

let d: orders.Draft = { note: "x" }     // Draft and note are not public</code></pre>
<pre><code>error BYN2005: `Draft` is not visible here
  --&gt; src/app/main.byn:3:15

  3 │ let d: orders.Draft = { note: "x" }
    │               ^^^^^ not `public`
    │
  help: mark `Draft` `public` to use it from another module

error BYN2005: `note` is not visible here
  --&gt; src/app/main.byn:3:25

  3 │ let d: orders.Draft = { note: "x" }
    │                         ^^^^ not `public`
    │
  help: mark `note` `public` to use it from another module</code></pre>

<h2 id="private">Hide a field with private</h2>
<p><code>private</code> is narrower than a module: the field is visible to its own type's methods, not even to a function in the same file. Use it for a value whose rules the type must keep; here an account's balance grows only through <code>deposit</code>.</p>
<example name="book-11-private"/>
<p>Reading <code>a.cents</code> from <code>main</code> is rejected. Add this line and run again:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">    print($"{a.cents}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2005:</span> `cents` is not visible here
  --&gt; book-11-private.byn:19:16

  19 │     print($"{a.cents}")
     │                ^^^^^ `private` to `Account`

✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings
  explain: bayan explain BYN2005</pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>The message names the type that owns the field; the fix is a <code>public</code> method such as <code>balance()</code>. Building the struct from outside (<code>Account { cents: 5, … }</code>) fails too: the literal names the field, and <code>open</code> is the only way in, so every account starts at zero.</p>
<note>The specification gives tests in a sibling <code>x.test.byn</code> file private access; the compiler does not, so such a test gets the same BYN2005. Test through the type's public methods.</note>

<h2 id="nested">Modules inside modules</h2>
<p>A directory below another module is its own module, imported by its full path below <code>src/</code>. Add <code>src/orders/orders.byn</code>, which imports <code>money</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">import money

public struct Order {
    public string id
    public money.Money price
}

public string describe(Order o) uses alloc =&gt; $"{o.id}: {o.price.total()} cents"</code></pre>
<p><code>describe</code> builds a string, so it declares <code>uses alloc</code> (chapter 14). Now add <code>src/orders/lines/lines.byn</code> with <code>public i32 limit() =&gt; 10</code>. <code>orders/lines/</code> is the module <code>orders.lines</code>: import it by that path and use the last segment as its name.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">import money
import orders
import orders.lines

let price = money.Money.dollars(3).adding(money.Money.dollars(4))
let o: orders.Order = { id: "A-1", price: price }
print(orders.describe(o))
print($"limit {lines.limit()}")</code></pre>
<p>Nesting only organises names: importing <code>orders</code> gives no <code>lines</code>. Use <code>lines.limit()</code> without <code>import orders.lines</code> and the compiler says <code>`lines` is not defined</code> (BYN2001). Run from the project root:</p>
<pre><code>bayan run</code></pre>
<pre><code>A-1: 700 cents
limit 10</code></pre>
<p>In an app you can omit <code>main</code>: statements go at the top of the entry file, as the tutorial did.</p>

<h2 id="rules">What the compiler rejects</h2>
<h3 id="unknown">An unknown module</h3>
<p>An import names a directory; with none, the compiler says where to put it.</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2004:</span> no module `mony`

  1 │ import mony
    │        ^^^^
    │
  help: put the module's `.byn` files in `mony/` next to the entry file; a module is a directory</pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler, from <code>tests/cases/diag/unknown-module</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<h3 id="entry">Statements outside the entry module</h3>
<p>A statement that is not a declaration belongs in the entry module. Put <code>print("hi")</code> in <code>src/orders/</code> and you get:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2015:</span> top-level statements are allowed only in the entry module
  --&gt; src/orders/hidden.byn:1:1

  1 │ print("top level in a module")
    │ ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^

✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings
  explain: bayan explain BYN2015</pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Two such files in one directory fail the same way, so scratch programs do not build as one.</p>
<h3 id="cycles">Import cycles</h3>
<p>If <code>a</code> imports <code>b</code> and <code>b</code> imports <code>a</code>, neither can be compiled first, and the compiler names the loop:</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2010:</span> import cycle: a → b → a
  --&gt; b/b.byn:1:8

  1 │ import a
    │        ^
    │
  help: move the shared declarations into a module both can import</pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler, from <code>tests/cases/diag/import-cycle</code>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<h3 id="duplicates">The same name twice</h3>
<p>Declaring <code>grade</code> in two files of one module is error BYN2006, <code>`grade` is declared twice</code>; the message points to both places.</p>

<h2 id="tests">Tests and modules</h2>
<p>Chapter 12 covers tests; two facts matter here. A test file next to a module's sources, like <code>src/money/money.test.byn</code>, belongs to that module and sees its names without an import:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">test "dollars are hundreds of cents" {
    assert(Money.dollars(2).total() == 200)
}</code></pre>
<p>Files in <code>tests/</code> join the entry module but still need their own imports: without <code>import money</code> in <code>tests/app.test.byn</code> the name is unknown.</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2001:</span> `money` is not defined
  --&gt; tests/app.test.byn:3:15

  3 │     let sum = money.Money.dollars(1).adding(money.Money.dollars(2))
    │               ^^^^^</pre>
<figcaption>Real output from the compiler. The compiler reports each use of <code>money</code>; the first is shown.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Run <code>bayan test</code>; both files run, reported by module name:</p>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="bayan test -v">running 2 tests in 2 modules
  ◆ money: dollars are hundreds of cents
  ◆ app: adds dollars

◆ 2 passed · 0.69s</code></pre>

<h2 id="build">Build and the project layout</h2>
<p><code>bayan build</code> in the project root compiles the app. It writes to <code>out/&lt;mode&gt;/&lt;target&gt;/</code>:</p>
<pre><code>out/safe/macos-arm64/tidy         the program
out/safe/macos-arm64/tidy-test    the test program
out/safe/macos-arm64/c/           the C that was generated
out/test/results.json             the last bayan test results</code></pre>
<p><code>out/</code> is disposable and ignored by git; <code>bayan clean</code> removes it. Compiled programs live in one cache per machine, so a second build of the same code, even in another checkout, compiles nothing.</p>
<p>A <em>library</em> has no program. <code>bayan new stock --lib</code> puts its code in <code>src/stock/stock.byn</code>, making <code>stock</code> the imported module, and its tests in <code>tests/stock.test.byn</code>. Building or running one is refused:</p>
<pre><code>bayan: `local/stock` is a library, so there is no program to run; `bayan test` runs its tests</code></pre>
<p>The package name is <code>local/&lt;name&gt;</code> until you publish it under a verified namespace. A project directory is named in kebab-case (<code>my-app</code>) or camelCase.</p>
<note>Planned: not in the compiler yet. The specification describes dependencies (<code>deps</code> in the manifest, minimal version selection, a lock file with content hashes, a registry with signed provenance) and a <code>public</code> meaning "visible to other packages", not only other modules. Today <code>public</code> serves the same project, and only <code>deps: []</code> builds.</note>

<h2 id="quiz">Check your understanding</h2>
<details class="quiz"><summary>Two files in <code>src/money/</code> each declare a function called <code>format</code>. What happens?</summary>
<p>BYN2006, <code>`format` is declared twice</code>: the files share one set of names, so neither shadows the other (<a href="#directory">details</a>).</p>
</details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>A struct has <code>private i32 cents</code>. A free function in the same file reads <code>a.cents</code>. Does it compile?</summary>
<p>No: BYN2005, "`cents` is not visible here", <code>`private` to `Account`</code>; <code>private</code> is narrower than the file (<a href="#private">details</a>).</p>
</details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>You write <code>print("loaded")</code> at the top of <code>src/orders/orders.byn</code>. What do you get?</summary>
<p>BYN2015, "top-level statements are allowed only in the entry module": only <code>src/app/</code> may hold statements, so it is an error, not a warning, and runs never.</p>
</details>
<details class="quiz"><summary><code>tests/app.test.byn</code> calls <code>money.Money.dollars(1)</code> and has no <code>import money</code>, although <code>src/orders/orders.byn</code> imports it. Does it compile?</summary>
<p>No: BYN2001, "`money` is not defined"; an import applies to the file that writes it, so the one in <code>orders</code> does not carry over.</p>
</details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>The module <code>a</code> imports <code>b</code> and <code>b</code> imports <code>a</code>. What does the compiler do?</summary>
<p>It stops with BYN2010, "import cycle: a → b → a", suggesting a third module both import. It does not pick an order or merely warn.</p>
</details>

